WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.313
Warn shows the user the
block, with a way to proceed.

2
00:00:03.313 --> 00:00:07.880
Microsoft Learn's rules overview
says warn needs Windows ten, version

3
00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:13.000
eighteen oh nine or later, and a recent
Defender Antivirus platform and engine.

4
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.898
It isn't available in
Configuration Manager,

5
00:00:15.898 --> 00:00:19.188
and on older Windows a rule
set to warn acts as block.

6
00:00:19.188 --> 00:00:21.075
Warn isn't a safety valve, though.

7
00:00:21.075 --> 00:00:24.772
Since platform four point
eighteen point two six oh six oh,

8
00:00:24.772 --> 00:00:29.471
using Unblock on a warn rule needs
administrator approval, and Microsoft

9
00:00:29.471 --> 00:00:34.400
says a persistent need belongs in a
per-rule exclusion, not repeated unblocks.

10
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.995
So brief the help desk
before the warn ring;

11
00:00:36.995 --> 00:00:41.295
the guide's RACI makes the help desk
responsible for unblock requests

12
00:00:41.295 --> 00:00:45.150
and user impact, and the policy
owner accountable for the rings.

13
00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:49.425
Two rules don't support warn at all,
and go from audit straight to block.

14
00:00:49.425 --> 00:00:54.388
One blocks credential stealing from the
Windows local security authority subsystem.

15
00:00:54.388 --> 00:00:59.210
The other blocks Office applications from
injecting code into other processes.
